# Copy to .env (gitignored). Every value has a working default.

# --- The stack (compose.yaml) ---------------------------------------------

# Station folders: one per frequency, named in stations.js. The folder is
# the station: audio files, plus any extras named after a track. The
# frequency names the variable, a dot becoming an underscore (59.1). The build
# hashes it into the station's .torrent, the deploy uploads it to the
# mirror, and the stack seeds it.
STATION_59_1="/path/to/59 Songs You MUST Hear Before You Die"

# A server without the folder leaves it unset: the seeder then fetches the
# station, its .torrent from the deployed site and its songs from the swarm
# and the mirror, into the stations volume, and seeds it from there (the web
# seed serves it too, once whole). It asks the site for a new version every
# STATION_REFRESH hours.
# SITE_URL=https://src.eco
# MIRROR_URL=https://radio.src.eco
# STATION_REFRESH=6

# Localhost by default. For LAN access, use this computer's LAN IP.
BIND_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1
WEBSEED_PORT=8001
TRACKER_PORT=8002

# Chats the stack's seeder sits in and relays, and the library answers in,
# comma-separated.
CHATS=trade

# Test peers for the ghost text's swarm memory (docker compose --profile
# test up -d --build peers, or node main.js --peers 8). They join PEER_CHATS
# and remember PEER_DATA: tinystories (children's stories) or quijote (Don
# Quijote, in Spanish). Open the site at ?chat=<PEER_CHATS> to meet them.
# PEERS=8
# PEER_DATA=tinystories
# PEER_CHATS=memorytest
# To try a flaky room: the share of questions left unanswered, and the
# most ms an answer is held back.
# PEER_DROP=0.5
# PEER_DELAY=400

# A public server, with TLS: Traefik in front of the tracker and the web
# seed, with certificates from Let's Encrypt (npm run public, the public
# profile). Point each hostname's DNS
# at the server and open TCP 80 and 443. ACME_EMAIL is optional. While
# trying it out, use Let's Encrypt's staging server, which has no rate
# limits but issues untrusted certificates:
# ACME_CA=https://acme-staging-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory
# One name can serve both (WebSocket upgrades go to the tracker):
# TRACKER_HOST=tracker.src.eco
# SEED_HOST=seed.src.eco
# ACME_EMAIL=
# PUBLIC_HTTP_PORT=80
# PUBLIC_HTTPS_PORT=443
#
# The library (public profile): peers that remember the prose and code of
# git repositories, and answer the ghost text's memory questions in CHATS,
# so what they hold sways what visitors are offered. PEER_SOURCES lists the
# repositories (commas, spaces or lines; a URL may end in #branch); each is
# cloned shallow, read for its prose and code (no binaries, lockfiles,
# vendored or built files, or files over 256 KB), and read again every
# PEER_REFRESH hours. PEER_WEIGHT (0 to 1) is how much an answer counts; a
# visitor's own counts 1. Each server running the library votes on its own,
# so two servers with the same sources count twice: split the sources
# between them, or lower the weight. With no sources, the library idles.
# It is one peer, so a visitor connects to it once and gets one answer: the
# text is indexed in slices of PEER_MB million characters (up to 16), and
# the peer answers from all of them at once.
# PEER_SOURCES=https://github.com/owner/repo,https://gitlab.com/group/project#main
# PEER_WEIGHT=1
# PEER_REFRESH=6
# PEER_MB=2
#
# The library also answers privately, for the browser extension's ghost
# text in other sites' fields: the extension asks without the library
# learning what was typed (next/private-memory.md). PEER_PRIVATE_GROUPS is
# the private table's size (4: about 900,000 characters held well; each
# extension downloads 1.3 MB per group from each library, once per
# version). PEER_KEY is a secret the library proves to the extension: its
# public key is logged at start; list the libraries' public keys in
# SRC_PINNED_KEYS (commas) where the extension is built, and it counts them
# above other peers (browsers answer too, so none is needed). PEER_PRIVATE=0
# turns private answers off.
# PEER_PRIVATE_GROUPS=4
# PEER_KEY=
# SRC_PINNED_KEYS=
#
# The seeder and the library use the host's network, so browsers reach them
# at the machine's own address (on a public server, with no TURN relay).
# Open WEBRTC_PORTS and PEER_WEBRTC_PORTS (UDP) in a public server's
# firewall and the cloud provider's. Each peer connection,
# and each offer waiting for an answer, holds one port; past the range's
# size, new connections are refused. UPLOAD_LIMIT caps what the seeder
# uploads, in KiB/s (0: no cap); the swarm makes up the rest.
# Behind a VPN that forwards one port (Proton through gluetun), set one port
# instead of a range: every connection then shares it. WEBRTC_PORT_FILE
# reads the port from a file (gluetun's forwarded_port) and restarts the
# peer when it changes; give the seeder and the library a connection each.
# WEBRTC_PORTS=51000-51999
# PEER_WEBRTC_PORTS=52000-52999
# UPLOAD_LIMIT=0

# Optional: a Cloudflare Tunnel token, for docker compose --profile tunnel.
# In the tunnel's settings, route a hostname to http://webseed:8001; it
# becomes SRC_SEED_URL below. Publishing the tracker too (a hostname to
# http://tracker:8002, as SRC_TRACKER_URL) is optional: the public trackers
# already connect peers, and this only adds one that does not depend on them.
# TUNNEL_TOKEN=

# --- The site (main.js) ---------------------------------------------------

# Where browsers find the stack, if anywhere: nothing points at it unless
# set (the stack on this machine is http://localhost:8001 and
# ws://localhost:8002). A deploy build leaves localhost addresses out. The
# stack does not have to be public, or running: the build makes each station's .torrent from its folder and
# bundles it into the site, and visitors get the songs from other listeners
# (and the seeder, when it is up) through the public trackers, and from the
# mirror below. A public SRC_SEED_URL adds the web seed as a source.
# SRC_SEED_URL=https://seed.src.eco
# SRC_TRACKER_URL=wss://tracker.src.eco
# More trackers, comma-separated, such as every public server's; visitors
# announce chats and stations to these too, before the public trackers:
# SRC_TRACKERS=wss://59.src.eco,wss://95.src.eco

# Cloudflare Pages, for node main.js --deploy (npm run deploy) and
# --undeploy (npm run undeploy). The token needs Pages: Edit, and for a
# custom domain, DNS: Edit and Cache Purge on its zone. With a domain set,
# the deploy points that domain's CNAME at Pages (taking over one that
# exists), and undeploy deletes it. A project serves
# one site: both refuse a project that serves another custom domain (the
# src-eco project serves src.eco), so test on dev.src.eco with its own.
# CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=
# CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=
# CLOUDFLARE_PAGES_DOMAIN=dev.src.eco
# CLOUDFLARE_PAGES_PROJECT=src-dev

# The radio's mirror (optional; scripts/mirror.js): every station's files in
# a Cloudflare R2 bucket at a domain of its own, which browsers fall back to
# only when no peer has a song (none turns up within 6 s, a song does not
# start within 10 s, or playback stalls for 5 s). Set both or neither. The
# deploy creates the bucket, its CORS and the domain (a name of one level
# under the zone, so the zone's certificate covers it: radio-dev.src.eco,
# not radio.dev.src.eco), checks each station folder (STATION_59_1 above)
# against its .torrent, and uploads only what the bucket lacks; undeploy
# removes it. R2 has to be enabled once in the dashboard, and the token
# needs Account > Workers R2 Storage > Edit. Free while the stations fit in
# 10 GB, and R2 charges nothing for downloads.
# CLOUDFLARE_R2_BUCKET=src-dev-radio
# CLOUDFLARE_R2_DOMAIN=radio-dev.src.eco
#
# Or any server that holds <info hash>/<station>/<path> (dev builds too):
# SRC_MIRROR_URL=https://radio.example.org

# The chat's relay (scripts/turn.js, functions/api/turn.js): Cloudflare's
# TURN service, so two browsers behind strict NATs (a phone on a cellular
# network, often) still chat. On unless turned off. The deploy makes a TURN
# key and an API token that can only read the account's analytics, keeps
# both secrets in the Pages project, never here, and undeploy deletes them.
# The site's /api/turn mints credentials that live an hour, and none while
# the account's TURN egress over the last CAP_DAYS days is at the cap. The
# window slides: 90 GB in any 3 days is 930 GB at most in a month, under
# Cloudflare's free 1,000 GB, and a spike stops the relay early; it can
# overshoot by what is relayed in the hour after the cap. Only chat uses it, never the
# radio. The token needs Realtime Admin (or Calls Write) and Account API
# Tokens: Edit; without them the deploy warns and chat connects directly.
# CLOUDFLARE_TURN=off
# CLOUDFLARE_TURN_CAP_GB=90
# CLOUDFLARE_TURN_CAP_DAYS=3
# CLOUDFLARE_TURN_TTL=3600
#
# Alerts about Cloudflare limits, posted to a Discord channel as "The
# Source": when the relay reaches half its cap, when it reaches the cap and
# stops, and when it cannot read its usage (and so hands out nothing). Each
# alert is sent about once per window, not on every request. Make the hook
# in Discord: the channel's settings, Integrations, Webhooks, New Webhook,
# Copy Webhook URL. The deploy keeps it in the Pages project as a secret.
# CLOUDFLARE_ALERT_WEBHOOK=https://discord.com/api/webhooks/...

# --- The browser extension (scripts/extension.js, scripts/stores.js) -------

# node main.js --extension builds it into build/extension/: a folder per
# browser to load unpacked (chrome://extensions with developer mode on,
# "Load unpacked"; or Firefox's about:debugging, "This Firefox", "Load
# Temporary Add-on", picking its manifest.json) and the zips the stores
# take. The site it belongs to: its homepage, and the chat's relay
# (/api/turn there). Firefox's id for it is permanent once published.
# SRC_SITE_URL=https://src.eco
# FIREFOX_EXTENSION_ID=src@src.eco
#
# node main.js --publish chrome|firefox|all uploads it (shows the plan and
# asks first; raise "version" in package.json before each release).
#
# Chrome Web Store: the first upload, the listing and the privacy answers
# are made by hand in the developer dashboard (the API cannot create an
# item); from then on, the item's id, the publisher id (Account in the
# dashboard), and an OAuth client with a refresh token for the scope
# https://www.googleapis.com/auth/chromewebstore (Google Cloud console:
# enable the Chrome Web Store API, make an OAuth client of type "Desktop
# app", and get a refresh token for it, e.g. with the OAuth Playground).
# CHROME_PUBLISH=staged holds an approved version for publishing by hand.
# CHROME_EXTENSION_ID=
# CHROME_PUBLISHER_ID=
# CHROME_CLIENT_ID=
# CHROME_CLIENT_SECRET=
# CHROME_REFRESH_TOKEN=
# CHROME_PUBLISH=staged
#
# addons.mozilla.org: an API key and secret from
# https://addons.mozilla.org/developers/addon/api/key/. The first upload
# makes the listing too (summary and description from
# src/text/en/extension.md).
# AMO_JWT_ISSUER=user:12345:67
# AMO_JWT_SECRET=
